.TH HALCYON-SWITCH 1 "November 2026" "Halcyon 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
halcyon-switch \- select Halcyon in PAT or VarAC, or restore the previous modem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B halcyon-switch
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.RI { status | on | off | varac | use " NAME" | list | host }
.SH DESCRIPTION
PAT and VarAC reach a modem over two loopback TCP ports, 8300 for commands and
8301 for data.  In PAT's configuration file the transport that carries the
Winlink packet protocol over those ports is called \fBvarahf\fR.
.B halcyon-switch
writes that block for Halcyon, saves the value that was there before, and can
start or stop the host service for you.  VarAC keeps its connection settings in
its own interface, so for VarAC the tool starts the host, asks it to identify
itself and prints the values to enter there.
.PP
The first switch copies the PAT configuration to
\fIconfig.json\fR\fB.halcyon\-backup\fR and records the previous transport in
\fIconfig.json\fR\fB.halcyon\-state.json\fR.  Running \fBon\fR again keeps the
first recorded value, and \fBoff\fR puts that value back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
PAT configuration to edit.  Defaults to
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pat/config.json\fR, or \fI~/.config/pat/config.json\fR.
The environment variable \fBHALCYON_PAT_CONFIG\fR overrides the default.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the transport block PAT currently holds, whether it points at Halcyon,
whether the host is running, and where the backup and log live.
.TP
.BI on
Write the \fBvarahf\fR block, then start the host service on 127.0.0.1:8300
and 127.0.0.1:8301 unless \fB\-\-no\-host\fR is given.
.TP
.B off
Stop the host service started by this tool and restore the previous transport
block.  With \fB\-\-keep\-host\fR the service is left running.
.TP
.BI varac
Start the host service if needed, send \fBVERSION\fR to the command port, and
print the address, port and bandwidth to use in VarAC's modem settings.  Exits
non-zero when nothing answers.  PAT's configuration is not touched.
.TP
.BI use " " \fINAME\fR
Apply a named profile from \fI~/.config/halcyon/modems.json\fR.  The built-in
\fBhalcyon\fR profile is always available and can be overridden by that file.
.TP
.B list
Print the available profiles.
.TP
.B host
Run the host service in the foreground; the same as \fBhalcyon-host\fR.
.SH "ON AND VARAC OPTIONS"
.TP
.BR \-\-mycall " " \fICALL\fR
Callsign reported to the client.  Default \fBN0CALL\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-bandwidth " " \fI500|2300|2750\fR
Bandwidth advertised to the client.  Default \fB2300\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-addr " " \fIHOST:PORT\fR
Address written into the PAT configuration (\fBon\fR only).  Default
\fB127.0.0.1:8300\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-host " " \fIADDRESS\fR
Address the host service binds.  Default \fB127.0.0.1\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-cmd\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
Command port.  Default \fB8300\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-data\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
Data port.  Default \fB8301\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-host
Do not start anything; \fBon\fR only edits the PAT configuration and \fBvarac\fR
only checks the running service.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would change without writing anything (\fBon\fR and \fBoff\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pat/config.json
PAT configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/halcyon/modems.json
Optional profile definitions, mapping a name to a transport and its settings.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/halcyon/host.log
Output of a host service started by \fBon\fR or \fBvarac\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Select Halcyon for PAT, then dial a gateway:
.PP
.nf
.RS
halcyon-switch on --mycall N0CALL
pat connect varahf:///N0CALL
halcyon-switch off
.RE
.fi
.PP
Start the modem for VarAC and print what to enter there:
.PP
.nf
.RS
halcyon-switch varac --mycall N0CALL
.RE
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR halcyon (1),
.BR halcyon-host (1),
.BR pat (1)
.PP
Setup guides installed with the package:
\fI/usr/share/doc/halcyon/PAT.md\fR and
\fI/usr/share/doc/halcyon/VARAC.md\fR.
